**The A1 row is inherited with the tier, not authored here** (`contract-agent § 3`). It arrives
precompiled, in citing form, and this project writes exactly **one** of its cells — **Shelf**. Every
other cell — its Roots included: the organisation's surfaces are the same in every project, and the
one that varies, its shelf, is a root by the Shelf cell — is the
projection of `system §A1`: rewriting it is self-scoping through the roster, and restating the
section instead of citing it is a copy that will drift. **`AGENT 0` never appears here** — it is
the reserved inbox address for *upstream*, not an actor (`contract-agent § 5`).

**The `Roots` column is the domain's ADDRESS IN THE TREE** (`contract-agent § 1` and `§ 3`):
backticked paths, each a folder (a trailing `/`) or a single file; **the most specific path wins**,
so a file root inside another actor's folder is legal. Every domain has one — mandatory; a surface
shared by nature has none and is a write-surface row below. Rooted by construction, never declared:
`.critos/system/`, every `.critos/memory/a<N>/`, the generated atlas, and **the folder of a declared
Shelf index** — an actor's shelf is its own by the Shelf cell (the steward's shelf included: the
one root of its row that varies per project arrives that way). The user rules a domain's
roots; the steward registers a root the ruled Domain already covers, and brings a border — a root
that widens a perimeter or enters another domain's ground — to the user. The roots check
(`/crit-doc-lint [22]`) reads every tracked file against this column and the surfaces table: a file
under no root and no ruled surface is a finding, so is a root declared twice, or one matching nothing.

**The Shelf column is an ADDRESS, not a guarantee** — the boot's awareness step
(`contract-session § 1`) opens it, so a **definition never has to name a document**. Its cell is a
**markdown link to the domain's index** (an empty shelf is `—`, legal): a shelf written in prose
cannot be verified, and the check that watches this column reads links only. Where the index is the
folder's `README.md`, list-completeness is machine-checked; any other entry point is legal, and its
completeness is the owner's.

**The `Form` column decides how an agent is reached**, and getting it wrong is silent: a `native`
agent is **invoked**; a `session` agent is **written to via the inbox**, because it is not running.

## Write surfaces — who holds the pen

The register of file-level pen-holding: where more than one domain's **work meets on one surface**,
the ruling that says whose hand writes. Naming this removes most inbox traffic on its own — on a
real project, 27% of all entries were "I touched your file, do not revert".

Rows come in **two kinds**, and the third column answers both honestly: **sole-hand** — exclusive
by ruling; *When a handoff is NOT needed* answers **never** — and **genuinely shared** — several
hands, the split stated. **Not an inventory:** a file nobody contests has its owner by domain
perimeter and no row here; a row exists only where a ruling was needed. A surface **shared by
nature** — a schema, a migrations folder, a jobs pocket several domains fill — has no root above
and lives here: its row is what the roots check reads as a ruled surface.

